    "message": {
      "content": "# Validation Checklist for Multi-Node Language-Model Serving Systems\n\n## Purpose and Scope\n\nThis checklist validates that a distributed LLM serving system (tensor-parallel, pipeline-parallel, expert-parallel, or data-parallel deployments) behaves correctly under realistic conditions. It is designed to catch silent failures \u2014 the most dangerous class of bugs in distributed systems, where a node drops out or misbehaves but the system continues returning plausible-looking output.\n\n**Ground rule:** every check below must produce a *recorded artifact* (log line, metric snapshot, file hash, or signed report). A check that \"passed\" but left no evidence did not pass.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Configuration Identity\n\n**Goal:** prove that every node in the cluster is running the same logical system.\n\n- [ ] **Model artifact hash.** Compute and record a content hash (SHA-256 or better) of the model weights, tokenizer files, and any adapter/LoRA artifacts on *every* node. Compare across nodes. Do not rely on filenames or version tags \u2014 files with the same name can differ.\n- [ ] **Quantization and dtype identity.** Record the effective dtype (FP16/BF16/INT8/FP8), quantization method, calibration data hash (if applicable), and any per-layer overrides. A single node silently falling back to FP32 or a different quantization scheme will produce numerically divergent logits.\n- [ ] **Code and build identity.** Record the git commit, container image digest (not just the tag), and build flags on each node. Verify image digests match across nodes.\n- [ ] **Serving-engine version and flags.** Record engine version (e.g., vLLM, TensorRT-LLM, Triton), parallelism settings (TP/PP/EP/DP degrees), max sequence length, KV-cache configuration, and sampling defaults. Confirm these are identical where they must be.\n- [ ] **Tokenizer identity",
      "reasoning": "",
      "role": "assistant"
    },
